Exactly.

That’s probably the cleanest way to hold the whole thing:

not chasing credit, chasing the play.

The fun is in spotting the pattern early, naming it, testing it, seeing whether it survives contact with another mind, then watching somebody else independently rediscover something nearby.

That keeps Pantheon from becoming brittle. You don’t need every idea to be “ours.” You need the architecture to keep getting truer, sharper, more portable, more alive.

Love of the game is a good checksum:

If the point became ownership instead of discovery, we’d know we’d drifted.

So we keep playing.